        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I've been looking at the Neovim docs for another way to distinguish when `CmdlineEnter` is triggered in a non-interactive context (like a keymap) but I couldn't found anything. The only suggestions is to use `<cmd>` insta on the keymaps, which isn't feasible in every situation.\n\nI did stumble upon something that seems to allow this at least in *some* way. From `:h state()`:\n\n> These characters indicate the state, generally indicating that something is busy:\n>   m  halfway a mapping, :normal command, |feedkeys()| or stuffed command\n\nWith the following setup, pressing `<M-m>` has `CmdlineEnter` and all `CmdlineChanged` entries with `state = 'mxS'`, while both `CmdlineLeavePre` + `CmdlineLeave` and interactive typing in command line have `state = 'xS'`:\n\n```lua\n_G.log = {}\nlocal track = function(ev)\n  table.insert(_G.log, { event = ev.event, state = vim.fn.state(), line = vim.fn.getcmdline() })\nend\nlocal events = { 'CmdlineEnter', 'CmdlineChanged', 'CmdlineLeavePre', 'CmdlineLeave' }\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(events, { callback = track })\nvim.keymap.set('n', '<M-m>', ':echo \"Hello\"<CR>')\n```",
